\define regexp-macro() (\w+)
\function f.inline(a) [tag[Task]] :filter[get[x]]
\procedure p.inline(a) go
\widget $my.inline(a) go
\define d.inline(a) go
\define $dollar.macro() one line
\widget $my.widget() a widget name requires a dollar
\procedure p.mvv(a:((var))) a multi-valued default stands here
\define d.mvv(a:((var))) and here the brackets belong to the body

\function f.block(a)
[tag[Task]] +[sort[title]]
\end

\define substitutes(a)
before $a$ and $(currentTiddler)$ and {{{ [tag[$a$]] }}} after
\end

\procedure leaves-them-literal(a)
before $a$ and $(currentTiddler)$ after
\end

! What this release changed, in one file

Every pragma above stands before any content, because TiddlyWiki reads pragmas only there.

!! A define body ending in a closing parenthesis

The first pragma closes on its own line. This paragraph, and the rest of the file, read as prose.

!! The four pragmas carry three languages

A function body reaches the filter engine. A procedure and a widget body read as wikitext with
their parameters arriving as variables. A define body reads as wikitext after substitution. The
one-line forms name their own kind, as the block forms already did.

!! Substitution belongs to the define body alone

Inside `substitutes` the dollars fire; inside `leaves-them-literal` they stay literal. Outside any
definition they stay literal too:

{{{ [tag[$a$]] }}}

!! A tag name takes what TiddlyWiki takes

<my-tag>a hyphen</my-tag> and <my.tag>a period</my.tag> stand as tags.

<my_tag>an underscore</my_tag> and <my:tag>a colon</my:tag> read as prose.

!! A tilde suppresses the link families TiddlyWiki suppresses

~CamelCase and ~[[a pretty link]] and ~https://example.com read suppressed.

!! Namespaced markup answers to the XML grammar

$$$image/svg+xml
<svg xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><dc:date>2012-05-10</dc:date></svg>
$$$
